                <pb facs="0412" n="405" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg405"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/9359: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-08-14T11:00:00+08:00">August 14, 1937—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received August 14—3 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>427. Your 146, August 13, 2 p.m. We made use of opportunity offered by visit to
                    Generalissimo last evening (see my 423, August 13, 8 p.m.) to emphasize
                    necessity for respecting neutrality of that part of Settlement patrolled by
                    international forces and Settlement police into which foreigners have been
                    drawn. Warning that Americans be <pb facs="0413" n="406" xml:id="pg406"/>
                    evacuated [from] Hongkew and Yangtzepoo areas indicates that Chinese are
                    prepared to respect neutrality of area south [of] Soochow Creek provided, of
                    course, that that area is not used by Japanese as a base for an attack upon
                    Chinese forces.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
